@+encoding: UTF-8

~ ============================================================
~ SLIM v2.0 — FORMAL SPECIFICATION
~ ============================================================

# 1. Overview

SLIM (Structured LLM Instruction Markup) is a plain-text file format designed for AI
documentation, agent configuration, and prompt engineering. It replaces Markdown as the
primary format for files consumed by Large Language Models (LLMs) and AI orchestration
systems.

SLIM achieves an average 24.5% token reduction over Markdown on YAML agent configs and
4.4% on plain prose, measured on cl100k_base tokenizer (47-file benchmark, 2026-05-13).
We are actively working to improve these numbers — contributions welcome.

## 1.1 Design Goals

Token efficiency: minimize tokens sent to LLM without losing semantic precision
Semantic clarity: every structural element has unambiguous, single meaning
Dual-author: readable and writable by both humans and AI systems
Injection safety: hard section boundaries prevent prompt injection attacks
All-purpose: covers system prompts, agent configs, tool schemas, docs, orchestration
Parser-friendly: unambiguous grammar, no context-sensitive edge cases

## 1.2 Core Architecture — Two Zones

Every SLIM file is divided into two strictly ordered zones:

::ZONE_DIAGRAM ascii
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  HEADER ZONE  (@key: value lines)                   │
│  Consumer: Orchestrator/parser only                 │
│  LLM sees: NO (stripped before LLM call)            │
│  Exception: @+ prefixed lines → LLM sees YES        │
├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│  CONTENT ZONE  (sections, prose, directives)        │
│  Consumer: LLM (primary) + parser (variables)       │
│  LLM sees: YES (after variable interpolation)       │
│  Structure: ::NAME sections, :::NAME nested         │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

# 2. Header Zone

## 2.1 Syntax

The header zone begins at the first line of the file and ends at the first line that is
neither an @-prefixed line, a blank line, nor a --- separator. A line beginning with ::
also terminates the header zone.

```
@key: value
@+key: value
@include: path
```

Rules:
Key must be lowercase ASCII: [a-z][a-z0-9_-]*
Separator: first ": " (colon + space). Value may contain subsequent colons.
Multi-line value: continuation with 2-space indent on next line(s)
Header zone terminates at first # heading, :: section, or non-blank non-@ line

## 2.2 Strip Semantics

| Prefix | LLM sees? | Variable interpolated? | Notes |
| @key   | No        | Yes ($key → value)     | Orchestrator-only metadata |
| @+key  | Yes       | Yes ($key → value)     | Context the LLM needs |

This is the primary token efficiency mechanism. Configuration metadata never reaches LLM.

## 2.3 Reserved Header Keys

| Key         | Type      | Description |
| @slim       | str       | Format version declaration. Omit for plain files (v2 assumed). |
| @agent      | str       | Agent identifier (use @+agent to send to LLM) |
| @model      | str       | Target model. Never sent to LLM (@model only). |
| @mode       | str       | Execution mode |
| @retry      | int       | Retry limit for orchestrator |
| @timeout    | str       | Timeout duration (e.g. 30s, 5m) |
| @tags       | list<str> | Metadata tags for indexing |
| @include    | url/path  | Import another .slm file (resolved first) |
| @version    | str       | Document version |

Custom keys are permitted. All keys parsed as typed values (see Section 6).

## 2.4 Type Coercion

Header values are automatically typed:

| Value Pattern | Inferred Type | Example |
| true / false  | bool          | @async: true |
| Integer       | int           | @retry: 3 |
| Decimal       | float         | @threshold: 0.7 |
| null / none   | null          | @context: null |
| a, b, c       | list<str>     | @stack: python, js, go |
| "42"          | str (forced)  | @id: "42" |
| Anything else | str           | @name: SecurityBot |

## 2.5 @include Directive

```
@include: ./base/agent.slm
@include: ./tools/mcp_tools.slm
```

Resolved by parser before any other processing.
Included file's @+ headers merge into current file's LLM context.
Included file's @ headers are defaults (current file overrides).
If both files define a ::NAME section, current file's section wins (last-one-wins).
Circular includes: parse error.
Max include depth: 5 levels.
@include is always stripped (@+include is invalid).

## 2.6 Multi-Document Files

Use --- (triple dash on its own line) to separate independent documents in one file.
Each document is parsed independently with its own header zone.
--- is a document separator in SLIM — not a horizontal rule.
In mdToSlm conversion, Markdown --- horizontal rules become blank lines, never ---.

```
@slim: 2.0
@+agent: Planner
# Role
You plan tasks.
---
@slim: 2.0
@+agent: Executor
# Role
You execute tasks.
```

# 3. Content Zone

## 3.1 Structural Elements

| Sigil      | Meaning              | Scope    |
| #          | H1 section heading   | Content  |
| ##         | H2 subsection        | Content  |
| ###        | H3 deep section      | Content  |
| ::NAME     | Named section (open) | Content  |
| :::NAME    | Nested section       | Content  |
| >KEYWORD   | Directive command    | Content  |
| $key       | Variable reference   | Content  |
| \|col\|    | Table row            | Content  |
| ~          | Comment (stripped)   | Anywhere |
| \X         | Literal escape       | Anywhere |

## 3.2 Directives

Directives are lines beginning with > followed immediately by a reserved ALL_CAPS keyword.
No space between > and keyword is required; one space is canonical.

Reserved keywords: CALL, ASSERT, YIELD, EMIT, LOG, ABORT, WAIT, RETRY

```
> CALL tool_name(param: value, param2: $var)
> ASSERT $result.status == complete
> YIELD $result.report
> EMIT event_name(payload: $data)
> LOG "Step completed"
> ABORT "Reason for stopping"
> WAIT 5s
> RETRY 3
```

A > followed by anything other than a reserved keyword is treated as literal text.
Example: "> 90% of bugs..." — not a directive.

## 3.3 Variable References

$key references are interpolated by the parser before the content is sent to the LLM.

$key — simple reference (must match a header key, case-sensitive)
$result.field — runtime dotpath reference (resolved by orchestrator at runtime)
$list[0] — indexed reference (runtime)
Literal dollar sign: \$amount → "$amount" verbatim
Variables in values containing newlines: only first line used

## 3.4 Tables

Table rows use pipe-delimited syntax. No separator row required (unlike Markdown).
First row is treated as the header. Saves ~6 tokens per table vs Markdown.

```
| Format | Tokens | Readable |
| JSON   | 340    | Medium   |
| SLIM   | 175    | High     |
```

Empty cells: || (adjacent pipes).

## 3.5 Inline Formatting

Inline formatting markers are stripped from LLM-facing output by all SLIM parsers.
Authors may write formatted SLIM-native files; parsers strip markers before sending to LLM.

Stripped markers: ** * _ ` (bold, italic, inline code)

```
**important** → important
_note_        → note
`git push`    → git push
```

No links in v2. URLs should be placed in named sections when needed.
No inline images (use ::BLOCK_NAME base64:png for images).

## 3.6 Comments

Lines starting with ~ (tilde) are stripped by all SLIM parsers before any output.
They never reach the LLM or the orchestrator. Use for author notes, TODOs, disabled lines.

```
~ Author: Jane Doe
~ TODO: add edge case for empty input
~ @+agent: DisabledAgent  ← this line is never parsed
```

# 4. Section Syntax

## 4.1 Syntax

```
::SECTION_NAME
content line one
content line two

::SECTION_NAME type
verbatim typed content

:::NESTED_NAME
nested content
```

Rules:
Section names: SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE by convention; parser accepts any casing.
::NAME opens a section. Ends at the next :: section, EOF, or document separator (---).
:::NAME opens a nested section (max 1 level deep). Ends at next ::: or :: or EOF.
4 or more colons at line start: parse error.
A blank line must precede :: at top level (or it must be at file/document start).
::: inside an outer :: section does not require a blank line before it.
Section names must be unique within a document (including across @include'd files).

## 4.2 Verbatim vs Processed

The type annotation on a section determines how its content is processed:

| Condition                        | Mode       | Variable interpolation | Directives active |
| No type annotation               | Processed  | Yes                    | Yes               |
| Type: markdown, text, prose      | Processed  | Yes                    | Yes               |
| Type: code or data language      | Verbatim   | No                     | No                |

Verbatim types: python, js, ts, go, rust, java, sql, bash, json, yaml, csv,
               raw, ascii, html, xml, base64:*, and any unrecognised type.
Processed types: markdown, text, prose, and no annotation.

In verbatim sections, $variables are literal and > CALL is literal text.
Use verbatim for code, queries, configs, and any content where syntax must be exact.
Use processed (no type) for instructions, context, and prose the LLM should interpret.

## 4.3 Escape Rule

In all sections, \:: at the start of a line produces a literal ::.
This is the only backslash sequence processed in verbatim sections.
All other backslashes in verbatim sections are captured as literal characters.

Example — CSS pseudo-elements inside a verbatim block:

```
::STYLES css
body { color: red; }
\::before { content: ""; }
\::after  { content: ""; }
```

The parser outputs ::before and ::after to the LLM. The leading \ is consumed.

In processed sections, the full escape set applies:
\@ → @ literal
\$ → $ literal
\> → > literal
\:: → :: literal (line start)
\~ → ~ literal

## 4.4 Blank Line Requirement

:: is recognised as a section marker only when:
  Preceded by a blank line, OR
  At the start of the file, OR
  Immediately after a document separator (---), OR
  Immediately after the header zone ends.

This prevents accidental section breaks from :: appearing in prose or code.
Example: "The C++ scope resolution operator :: is used for namespace access."
         → mid-line, never triggers a section.
         → line-start without blank line before it: also safe.

::: does not require a blank line. It only appears inside an outer :: section,
where natural text starting with ::: at line start does not occur in practice.

## 4.5 Nesting

Nesting depth is limited to 1 level (:: → :::). No deeper nesting is valid.

```
::EXAMPLES

:::EXAMPLE_1
user: Is this SQL safe?
assistant: No. SQL injection on line 2.

:::EXAMPLE_2
user: How to fix it?
assistant: Use parameterized queries.
```

:::EXAMPLE_1 ends when :::EXAMPLE_2 opens.
:::EXAMPLE_2 ends when the next :: section opens, or at EOF.
::EXAMPLES ends when the next top-level :: section opens, or at EOF.

## 4.6 Special Types

| Type         | Verbatim | Meaning |
| python       | Yes      | Python source code |
| js           | Yes      | JavaScript source |
| ts           | Yes      | TypeScript source |
| json         | Yes      | JSON data |
| yaml         | Yes      | YAML data |
| sql          | Yes      | SQL query |
| bash         | Yes      | Shell commands |
| go           | Yes      | Go source code |
| rust         | Yes      | Rust source code |
| java         | Yes      | Java source code |
| html         | Yes      | HTML markup |
| xml          | Yes      | XML data |
| csv          | Yes      | Comma-separated data |
| ascii        | Yes      | ASCII art / diagrams |
| raw          | Yes      | Uninterpreted text |
| base64:png   | Yes      | Base64-encoded PNG image |
| base64:jpg   | Yes      | Base64-encoded JPEG image |
| markdown     | No       | Markdown prose (processed) |
| text         | No       | Plain prose (processed) |
| prose        | No       | Alias for text (processed) |

# 5. Schema Definitions

## 5.1 Syntax

Tool schemas define callable functions available to the agent.
Schema blocks begin with :tool_name (single colon prefix — distinct from :: sections).
Properties are 2-space indented below the tool name.

```
:tool_name
  desc: Human-readable description of what this tool does
  param1!: type
  param2?: type = default_value
  param3?: [option_a|option_b|option_c] = option_a
  -> return_field: type, return_field2: type
```

Note: :tool_name (single colon) defines a schema.
      ::SECTION_NAME (double colon) opens a content section.
      These are unambiguous to both parser and human reader.

Property suffixes:
! = required (parse error if missing at call time)
? = optional
= value = default value (implies optional)

## 5.2 Custom Type Definitions

Custom types are defined in named sections with a reserved name TYPE_DEF:

```
::TYPE_DEF
  TypeName:
    field1!: type
    field2?: type = default
```

Custom types can then be used in schema definitions: -> results: list<TypeName>

## 5.3 Complete Schema Example

```
::TYPE_DEF
  Finding:
    line!: int
    severity!: [low|medium|high|critical]
    message!: str
    fix?: str

:analyze_code
  desc: Run static analysis on a code snippet
  code!: str
  language?: [python|js|java|go|rust] = python
  ruleset?: [owasp|cwe|custom] = owasp
  depth?: int = 3
  -> score: float, findings: list<Finding>, summary: str
```

# 6. Type System

## 6.1 Primitive Types

| Type  | Description    | Examples |
| str   | UTF-8 string   | "hello", hello |
| int   | Integer        | 3, -10, 0 |
| float | Decimal        | 0.7, -1.5, 3.14 |
| bool  | Boolean        | true, false |
| null  | Null/None      | null, none |

## 6.2 First-Class Types

| Type     | Description          | Validation |
| datetime | ISO 8601 datetime    | 2026-05-14T14:30:00Z |
| url      | Valid URL            | https://example.com |
| uuid     | UUID v4              | 550e8400-e29b-41d4-a716 |
| json     | Arbitrary JSON object | Any valid JSON |

## 6.3 Collection Types

| Type       | Syntax     | Example |
| Enum       | [a\|b\|c]  | [prod\|staging\|dev] |
| List       | list<T>    | list<str>, list<Finding> |
| Dict       | dict<K,V>  | dict<str,int> |

## 6.4 User-Defined Types

Defined in ::TYPE_DEF sections. Reusable across schema definitions in the same file
or in files that @include this file.

# 7. Security Model

## 7.1 Zone Ordering Enforcement

The header zone MUST precede the content zone. Any @key found after the first #
heading, :: section, or non-blank non-@ line is treated as literal content text,
not a header. This prevents header injection.

## 7.2 Section Boundary Injection

Parsers must detect when user-provided content contains :: at the start of a line
(after a blank line) and treat it as an injection attempt in strict mode.
Use sanitize_user_content() on all user-provided strings before embedding.

## 7.3 Variable Value Sanitization

Variable values containing newline characters: only first line is used.
Parsers MUST provide sanitize_user_content(str) → str that escapes at line start:

  :: at line start → \::
  @               → \@
  $               → \$
  >               → \>
  ~               → \~

Implementation: split by \n, apply escapes per line, rejoin.
Mid-line occurrences of these characters are safe and must NOT be escaped.

## 7.4 @include Path Traversal

@include paths are resolved relative to the including file's directory.
Absolute paths: allowed but flagged by linters.
Network URLs: parser-implementation-dependent (disabled by default).
Circular includes: detect and raise parse error.

# 8. Parser Behaviour

## 8.1 Parse Modes

| Mode    | On error  | Use case |
| strict  | Raise exception with line number | Linters, CI validation |
| lenient | Collect warnings, return partial result | LLM-facing production parsers |

## 8.2 Output Structure

A parsed SLIM document produces:

```
SLIMDocument:
  version: str                       # from @slim header, default "2.0"
  headers: dict<str, any>            # all @ headers (stripped)
  llm_headers: dict<str, any>        # @+ headers (LLM-visible)
  sections: dict<str, Section>       # named :: sections by name
  nested: dict<str, list<Section>>   # ::: sections grouped by parent name
  schemas: dict<str, Schema>         # :tool definitions
  directives: list<Directive>        # > KEYWORD calls
  body_text: str                     # full content, variables interpolated
  llm_text: str                      # body_text minus ~ comments and @ header zone
```

## 8.3 Strip Mode

strip_mode=True removes @ headers and ~ comments before returning llm_text.
This is the text that should be sent to the LLM.
Inline formatting markers (* ** _ `) are stripped from llm_text in all modes.

## 8.4 Version Compatibility

| @slim value | Rules applied |
| absent      | v2 (current default) |
| 2.0         | v2 (:: sections, inline stripped) |
| 1.0         | v1 (=== blocks, inline passthrough) |

Parsers MUST support both v1 and v2 rules selected by the @slim header.

## 8.5 Round-Trip Canonical Form

slim fmt produces canonical form:
One blank line between top-level :: sections
No trailing whitespace
Section names in SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE
Schema properties: required (!) first, optional (?) second, alphabetical within each group
Consistent 2-space indentation in schemas

## 8.6 Whitespace Rules

| Rule |
| Indentation: spaces only. Tabs = parse error in schemas. |
| Blank lines between sections: one blank line canonical; multiple collapsed to one. |
| Blank lines inside verbatim sections: preserved. |
| Trailing spaces on all lines: stripped. |
| BOM (UTF-8 byte order mark): stripped silently. |

# 9. BNF Grammar

::BNF_GRAMMAR text
document       ::= header_zone content_zone
header_zone    ::= (header_line | blank_line)*
header_line    ::= at_key ": " value newline
                 | "@include" ": " path newline
at_key         ::= ("@" | "@+") [a-z][a-z0-9_-]*
content_zone   ::= (section | nested_section | schema | directive | table_row
                  | paragraph | comment | blank_line)*
section        ::= section_open section_content
section_open   ::= "::" SECTION_NAME (" " type)? newline
nested_section ::= nested_open nested_content
nested_open    ::= ":::" SECTION_NAME (" " type)? newline
section_content ::= (escaped_line | non_section_line)*
escaped_line   ::= "\::" text newline
schema         ::= ":" identifier newline property+
property       ::= "  " identifier ("!" | "?")? ": " type (" = " value)? newline
                 | "  " "->" " " return_spec newline
directive      ::= ">" " " KEYWORD (" " args)? newline
table_row      ::= "|" (cell "|")+ newline
paragraph      ::= text newline
comment        ::= "~" text? newline
variable_ref   ::= "$" identifier ("." identifier | "[" integer "]")*
SECTION_NAME   ::= [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_]*
KEYWORD        ::= "CALL"|"ASSERT"|"YIELD"|"EMIT"|"LOG"|"ABORT"|"WAIT"|"RETRY"
type           ::= identifier (":" identifier)?

# 10. File Metadata

| Property       | Value |
| Extension      | .slm |
| MIME type      | text/slim |
| Encoding       | UTF-8 (BOM optional, stripped) |
| Line endings   | LF preferred, CRLF tolerated |
| Max header val | 2048 characters |
| Max include depth | 5 levels |
| Max section nesting | 1 level (:: → :::) |
| Section name charset | ASCII letters, digits, underscore |

# 11. Migration Guide

## 11.1 From Markdown to SLIM v2

| Markdown | SLIM v2 | Notes |
| --- frontmatter | @key: value header | Use @+ to expose to LLM |
| # Heading | # Heading | Identical |
| - bullet | bare line | Strip the - prefix; saves 1 token per bullet |
| 1. step | bare line | Strip the 1. prefix |
| \`\`\`lang ... \`\`\` | ::BLOCK_NAME lang ... | Named + typed, no close tag |
| <!-- comment --> | ~ comment | Shorter |
| \| col \| col \| \n \|---\|---\| | \| col \| col \| | No separator row; saves ~6 tokens |
| *bold* / **bold** | stripped on output | No action needed; parser strips |
| `inline code` | stripped on output | No action needed; parser strips |
| --- horizontal rule | blank line | In mdToSlm: HR → blank line, never doc separator |

## 11.2 SLIM-Only Features (no Markdown equivalent)

@key header stripping (tokens never reach LLM)
$variable interpolation
> DIRECTIVE command syntax
:tool schema definitions with typed params
::NAME section type tags and verbatim mode
~ comments (stripped silently)
Section boundary injection protection
list<T>, dict<K,V>, [enum|values] types

## 11.3 Migration Steps (Markdown → SLIM v2)

1. Rename file from .md to .slm
2. Convert YAML frontmatter (---) to @key: / @+key: headers
3. Remove --- frontmatter delimiters
4. Replace ``` code blocks with ::BLOCK_NAME lang (no close tag needed)
5. Replace <!-- comments --> with ~ comments
6. Remove | --- | separator rows from all tables
7. Add @slim: 2.0 as first header line (or omit — v2 is assumed)
8. Run: slim validate yourfile.slm

## 11.4 Migrating from SLIM v1 to SLIM v2

| SLIM v1 | SLIM v2 | Notes |
| @slim: 1.0 | @slim: 2.0 | Update version declaration |
| === BLOCK_NAME [type] | ::BLOCK_NAME type | No brackets around type |
| === /BLOCK_NAME | (remove) | No close tag in v2 |
| === BLOCK_NAME | ::BLOCK_NAME | Untyped block |
| === /BLOCK_NAME | (remove) | |
| Nested === blocks | :: outer + ::: inner | |
| Inline *bold* passthrough | Stripped automatically | No change needed in files |
| sanitize: escapes === | sanitize: escapes :: at line start | Update calls |

Automated migration: slim migrate --from=1.0 --to=2.0 yourfile.slm

# 12. Versioning

| Version | Status  | Block syntax | Inline fmt | Notes |
| 1.0     | Legacy  | === NAME [type] ... === /NAME | Passthrough | Supported via @slim: 1.0 |
| 2.0     | Current | ::NAME type (no close) | Stripped | Default when @slim absent |
| 2.1     | Planned | TBD | | Conditional blocks, loop constructs |

# 13. Complete Reference Example

::FULL_EXAMPLE slim
@slim: 2.0
@include: ./base/code_reviewer.slm
@model: claude-opus-4-7
@retry: 3
@timeout: 60s
@+agent: SecurityBot
@+task: PR-942
@+env: production

~ Security review flow for incoming pull requests

# Role
You are $agent performing a security audit on $task in $env.

# Rules
Score risk 0.0-1.0. Above 0.7 blocks the PR automatically.
Cite exact line number for each finding.
Use [CRITICAL] / [HIGH] / [MEDIUM] / [LOW] labels inline.

# Steps
1. Read USER_CODE section below
2. > CALL analyze_risk(pr: $task, code: $input, env: $env)
3. > ASSERT $result.score != null
4. > YIELD $result.report

::USER_CODE python
def get_user(id):
    query = f"SELECT * FROM users WHERE id={id}"
    return db.execute(query)

::EXAMPLES

:::EXAMPLE_1
user: Is f"SELECT * WHERE id={id}" safe?
assistant: No. [CRITICAL] Line 2 — SQL injection. Use: db.execute("SELECT * WHERE id=?", (id,))

:::EXAMPLE_2
user: What severity is a missing input validation?
assistant: [HIGH] Missing validation enables injection. Always sanitize before queries.

::TYPE_DEF
  Finding:
    line!: int
    severity!: [low|medium|high|critical]
    message!: str
    fix?: str

:analyze_risk
  desc: Score a PR for security vulnerabilities
  pr!: str
  code!: str
  env!: [prod|staging|dev]
  ruleset?: [owasp|cwe|custom] = owasp
  depth?: int = 3
  -> score: float, findings: list<Finding>, summary: str
